The web app's bundle and the phone app include third-party code.

The web build compiles its runtime dependencies into the JavaScript and CSS it
ships, among them React, react-dom and scheduler (MIT, Meta Platforms, Inc.
and affiliates), Radix UI (MIT, WorkOS), lucide-react (ISC, Lucide Icons and
Contributors), class-variance-authority (Apache-2.0, Joe Bell), Firebase
(Apache-2.0, Google LLC), three.js, pdf.js (Apache-2.0, Mozilla Foundation)
with the core-js polyfills compiled into its legacy build (MIT, Denis Pushkarev
and CoreJS Company), pdf-lib, fflate, the noble cryptography libraries and the
Inter and JetBrains Mono typefaces (OFL-1.1) — and the packages those depend
on, among them the d3 modules (ISC; d3-ease BSD-3-Clause), pako (MIT and
Zlib), tslib (0BSD), zustand and Floating UI (MIT). The phone app carries
React Native and some seven hundred packages under it, among them jsQR and the
React Native Firebase modules (Apache-2.0), and links Google's native Firebase
SDKs, which are under Google's terms. Each keeps its own licence.

The licence texts travel with each app. The web build writes LICENSE.txt,
NOTICE.txt and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt beside the bundle, the last generated
from the packages the bundle contains (jojo:licence-notices in
web/vite.config.ts). The phone app shows the notices of its whole production
dependency tree on Guide > Built with, generated by
mobile/scripts/third-party-notices.mjs. The web Guide's "Built with" page lists
every package jojo imports by name with its installed version, licence and
copyright holder; the production build fails if it bundles a package that page
does not credit (jojo:credited-bundle in web/vite.config.ts), and a test fails
if a credited version or licence differs from node_modules
(web/src/components/guide/credits.test.ts).

This repository also keeps some third-party files, and files derived from
third-party work. They are not wholly jojo's own work, the Apache-2.0 grant
above does not cover what others wrote in them, and each comes under its own
terms: the robot model in
web/src/assets/jojo-robot.glb (CC BY 4.0, Jungle Jim, modified); the Inter and
JetBrains Mono font files in mobile/android/app/src/main/assets/fonts (OFL-1.1);
UI components in web/src/components/ui derived from shadcn/ui (MIT, shadcn);
the Spotlight component, derived from Aceternity UI, whose terms are not a
standard open-source licence and are still to be confirmed; the pdf.js
text-layer CSS copied into web/src/index.css (Apache-2.0, Mozilla Foundation,
modified); and agent skills under .agents/skills and .claude/skills, which are
development tooling, ship in neither app, and whose licences are recorded, or
recorded as not confirmed, beside them. The programs jojo TALKS TO —
MarkItDown, and whichever model server you point it at — are installed and run
by you on your own machine, and none of their code is in either app bundle.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md gives the terms, copyright lines and sources of all of
these.
